{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>Their calyxes and their branches shall be of it.</b> In Hekommetz Rabba (Menahot 28b) they taught: seven branches of the menorah are mutually indispensible [to one another]; its seven lamps are mutually indispensible. What is the reason? ״הֲוָיָה״ is written of them — thus far [the Gemara]. And it is difficult: for was ״הֲוָיָה״ said only concerning the branches and not concerning the lamps? For two instances of הֲוָיָה were stated regarding the menorah: one in this verse and one in the first verse (Exodus 25:31): ״וְעָשִׂיתָ מְנוֹרַת… יְרֵכָהּ וְקָנָהּ גְּבִיעֶיהָ כַּפְתּוֹרֶיהָ וּפְרָחֶיהָ מִמֶּנָּה יִהְיוּ״ — which implies that only the seven branches and its cups, its calyxes, and its blossoms are indispensible. And although in the Mishnah [the Tanna] mentioned only the branches and the lamps, the Tosafot wrote that the same applies to the rest [i.e., the cups, calyxes, and blossoms], and the Tanna did not mention them because they are not [relevant] in metal menorahs; and so the Rambam wrote in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Beit HaBeḥirah — see there. But regarding the lamps, [Scripture] did not mention הֲוָיָה concerning them. Now, there is a dispute among our Sages of blessed memory (Menahot 88b) whether the lamps were fixed [to the menorah] or not — and no difficulty arises according to the one who says they were not fixed, for they are vessels in their own right and we do not find הֲוָיָה concerning them. Rather, even according to the one who says they were fixed, he must concede that they are not hammered out of it [i.e., not of one piece with it], for our Sages of blessed memory did not disagree in chapter 2 of Middot (Menahot 88b) except concerning whether [the menorah was made] from the ingot or separately from the ingot — but that [the lamps] are not hammered from it is agreed by all. And likewise the verse was precise in saying ״מִקְשָׁה — hammered: its base, its branch, its cups, its calyxes, and its blossoms,״ but its lamps it did not say. And after [Scripture] stated the detailed rules of the menorah at length, it then said that its lamps shall be made — this clearly shows that they are separate from the menorah. Furthermore, the verse was precise in saying <b>מִמֶּנָּה יִהְיוּ</b>, to say: those that are מִמֶּנָּה — of it — it is upon them that I stipulate the [requirement of] הֲוָיָה." "Or perhaps, since the reason for the seven branches was revealed — that they are for placing lamps in them — if so, when there is no lamp, the branch is void and as if it does not exist, and it [the branch's absence] is therefore indispensible. Or perhaps [the reason is] that the Talmud in Hekommetz Rabba (Menahot 28b) did not ask ״What is the reason?״ except concerning the branches; but the lamps do not need [a separate reason], for these depend on those."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The lemma heading מִמֶּנָּה יִהְיוּ appears at the end of comment 0 rather than at its start, since the comment opens by quoting the fuller lemma כַּפְתֹּרֵיהֶם וּקְנֹתָם מִמֶּנָּה יִהְיוּ. The bold has been placed where the Or HaChaim himself bolds the shorter phrase, per the lemma head convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:33:44.605937560Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}